{:edb/db-before-t 1923 :edb/db-after-t 1924 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535236 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:11:17.893Z" 13194139535236 true] [17592186228864 1100 "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $yy' = x^2y + e^x$\n\n- $y = x\\ln(1+y') + (y')^2$\n\n- $y + \\dfrac{x}{1+y'} - y' = 0$" 13194139535236 true] [17592186228864 1100 "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n1. $yy' = x^2y + e^x$\n2. $y = x\\ln(1+y') + (y')^2$\n3. $y + \\dfrac{x}{1+y'} - y' = 0$" 13194139535236 false] [17592186228864 1145 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'$.\n\nNote that, in the special case where\n\n$$\nf(y') = y'\n$$\n\nthe equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y$, we get\n$y = \\dfrac{e^x}{y' - x^2}$,\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$.\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n$f(y') = \\ln(1+y'), \\qquad g(y') = (y')^2$.\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y$, we get\n$y = y' - \\dfrac{x}{1+y'}$,\nwhich is of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n$f(y') = -\\dfrac{1}{1+y'}, \\qquad g(y') = y'$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535236 false] [17592186228864 1145 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f(y') = y',$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{e^x}{y' - x^2},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y = xf(y') + g(y').$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n\n$$\nf(y') = \\ln(1+y'), \\qquad g(y') = (y')^2.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny = y' - \\dfrac{x}{1+y'},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y = xf(y') + g(y')$ with\n\n$$\nf(y') = -\\dfrac{1}{1+y'}, \\qquad g(y') = y'.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535236 true] [17592186228865 1100 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-1\\right)^2 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nIf $y'(x) = t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\left( \\dfrac{2\\ln|t| - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2} \\right) f(t) + g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}\n$$" 13194139535236 true] [17592186228865 1100 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$y = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-1\\right)^2 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)$.\n\nIf $y'(x) = t$, then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t))$, where\n$y(t) = \\left(\\dfrac{2\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2} \\right) f(t) + g(t)$\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t)$.\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}$" 13194139535236 false] [17592186228865 1145 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'$.\n\nWe're told that, under the substitution $y'(x) = t$, our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(t) &= e^{∫P(t)dt} \\\\\n&= e^{∫2/(t - 1)dt} \\\\\n&= e^{2\\ln \\mid t - 1 \\mid} \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln (t - 1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= (t - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(t - 1)^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt} + 2(t - 1)x &= (t - 1)^{2}(1 + t)/(t(1 - t)) \\\\\n(t - 1)^{2}\\frac{dx}{dt} + 2(t - 1)x &= \\frac{1 - t^{2}}{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}(x(t - 1)^{2}) &= \\frac{1 - t^{2}}{t} \\\\\n∫\\frac{d}{dt}(x(t - 1)^{2})dt &= ∫\\frac{1 - t^{2}}{t}dt \\\\\nx(t - 1)^{2} &= ∫\\frac{1}{t} - tdt \\\\\nx(t - 1)^{2} &= \\ln \\mid t\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + C \\\\ x|= (2\\ln \\mid t\\end{vmatrix}- t^{2} + C)/(2(t - 1)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{2\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2}\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny = x(t-1)^2 + 2t\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= ((2\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t^{2} + C)/(2(t - 1)^{2}))(t - 1)^{2} + 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(t)=(t-1)^2,\\ g(t)=2t\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t) = t^2 +1\n$$" 13194139535236 false] [17592186228865 1145 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution $y'(x) = t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{2\\ln|t| - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny = x(t-1)^2 + 2t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}})(t-1)^{2}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=(t-1)^2,\\ g(t)=2t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t) = t^2 +1.\n$$" 13194139535236 true] [17592186228884 1100 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$" 13194139535236 true] [17592186228884 1100 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n$y = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2$.\nUsing the substitution $t = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$, the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)$.\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q$." 13194139535236 false] [17592186228884 1145 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y')\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'$.\n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x = x(t)$. We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x$.\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin t + 5t^2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x\\sin t + 5t^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules, and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x\\sin t) + \\frac{d}{dx}(5t^{2}) \\\\\nt &= 2\\sin t\\frac{d}{dx}(x) + 2x\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin t) + 10t\\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\\nt &= 2\\sin t + 2x\\cos t\\frac{dt}{dx} + 10t\\frac{dt}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt - 2\\sin t &= (2x\\cos t + 10t)\\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\\n\\frac{t - 2\\sin t}{2x\\cos t + 10t} &= \\frac{dt}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}=\\dfrac{t-2\\sin t}{2x\\cos t+10t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=\\dfrac{2x\\cos t+10t}{t-2\\sin t}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{2x\\cos t + 10t}{t - 2\\sin t} \\\\\n(t - 2\\sin t)\\frac{dx}{dt} &= 2x\\cos t + 10t \\\\\n(t - 2\\sin t)\\frac{dx}{dt} - 2x\\cos t &= 10t \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} - \\frac{2\\cos t}{t - 2\\sin t}x &= \\frac{10t}{t - 2\\sin t} \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{2\\cos t}{2\\sin t - t}x &= \\frac{10t}{t - 2\\sin t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{2\\cos t}{2\\sin t-t}, \\qquad Q(t) = \\dfrac{10t}{t-2\\sin t}\n$$" 13194139535236 false] [17592186228884 1145 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right) + 5\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x = x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny = 2x\\sin t + 5t^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules, and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{t}^{2}) \\\\ t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+10t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+10t\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x}=\\dfrac{t-2\\sin t}{2x\\cos t+10t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=\\dfrac{2x\\cos t+10t}{t-2\\sin t}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\ (t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10t \\\\ (t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =10t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}x & =\\frac{10t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t}x & =\\frac{10t}{t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{2\\cos t}{2\\sin t-t}, \\qquad Q(t) = \\dfrac{10t}{t-2\\sin t}.\n$$" 13194139535236 true] [17592186229101 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339029 13194139535236 true] [17592186229101 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339036 13194139535236 true] [17592186229101 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339043 13194139535236 true] [17592186229101 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339050 13194139535236 true] [17592186229101 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339057 13194139535236 true] [17592186229102 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339099 13194139535236 true] [17592186229102 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339110 13194139535236 true] [17592186229102 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339121 13194139535236 true] [17592186229102 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339132 13194139535236 true] [17592186229102 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339143 13194139535236 true] [17592186229103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339064 13194139535236 true] [17592186229103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339071 13194139535236 true] [17592186229103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339078 13194139535236 true] [17592186229103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339085 13194139535236 true] [17592186229103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339092 13194139535236 true] [17592186339029 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339030 13194139535236 true] [17592186339029 1089 "q-337223" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339029 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535236 true] [17592186339029 1100 "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+({y}^{′})^{2})$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+1$\n- $y-x({y}^{′})^{2}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}=0$" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339029 1113 #uuid "103caf7c-e81f-5080-9c10-df7884ea851f" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339029 1145 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′}),\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+({y}^{′})^{2}).\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x({y}^{′})^{2}-{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339030 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339031 13194139535236 true] [17592186339030 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339032 13194139535236 true] [17592186339030 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339033 13194139535236 true] [17592186339030 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339034 13194139535236 true] [17592186339030 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339035 13194139535236 true] [17592186339030 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535236 true] [17592186339030 1041 "selection" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339030 1104 #edb/ref 17592186339032 13194139535236 true] [17592186339030 1110 #uuid "908e7516-da91-5c7c-9fa1-35a431b67efa" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339031 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339031 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535236 true] [17592186339031 1111 #uuid "11ddbceb-6b4b-5765-916d-c745dbb9d56c" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339032 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339032 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535236 true] [17592186339032 1111 #uuid "bfc513d0-eec4-5bd7-ac02-278bc05e2776" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339033 1034 "I only" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339033 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535236 true] [17592186339033 1111 #uuid "7b5ee0f6-499e-53b0-9a70-afc0bad17c86" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339034 1034 "III only" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339034 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535236 true] [17592186339034 1111 #uuid "f5ea762f-3e61-5622-b6da-c96293f6ff9c" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339035 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339035 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535236 true] [17592186339035 1111 #uuid "dd840250-49fa-5551-b384-1fa1c40fec2d" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339036 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339037 13194139535236 true] [17592186339036 1089 "q-337226" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339036 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535236 true] [17592186339036 1100 "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n\n- $y=x({y}^{′})^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′})$\n- $y+x({y}^{′})^{2}={2}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+x$" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339036 1113 #uuid "ffbf0fc3-7776-519a-bdcb-35221539c005" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339036 1145 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}).\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=-x({y}^{′})^{2}+{2}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-({y}^{′})^{2},\\,g({y}^{′})={2}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535236 true] [17592186339037 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339038 131941